* ===========================================================================
* A CDX TAG IS A FIELD NAME, AND THE INDEX LAYER NOW ASKS THE SAME RESOLVER
* EVERYONE ELSE ASKS (AIF-157 step 3, written 2026-09-06).
*
* WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. xfg::resolve_field_index_std (include/xbase_field_
* getters.hpp) is the house answer to "which field is this name". It trims,
* lets logical names win, and -- for x64 tables only -- accepts the generated
* 10-byte DBF descriptor token as an alias when it maps uniquely. Eleven files
* called it. NOTHING UNDER src/xindex/ DID. The index layer carried FOUR more
* opinions of its own:
*
*   index_manager.cpp     activeTagFieldIndex1()  upper-only, no trim, no alias
*   cdx_native_backend    field_index_for_tag_()  its own matcher
*   cnx_backend           field_index_for_tag_()  a byte-for-byte copy of it
*   dbarea_adapt.cpp      field_index_ci()        a seventh-hand version
*
* plus BUILDLMDB's own loop. All are now routed onto the standard resolver.
* Nothing in the corpus exercised what that changed, which is the hole this
* file fills.
*
* THE TWO CLAIMS UNDER TEST, and they are different mechanisms:
*
*   1. A TAG NAME LONGER THAN TEN BYTES. The CDX tag directory stores a name in
*      `char name[32]` (include/cdx/cdx.hpp:52), NUL-padded. Read back as a
*      string that is "STUDENT_LAST_NAME" followed by NULs, and trim_copy only
*      strips isspace -- NUL is not isspace -- so an untruncated name never
*      equals its own trimmed spelling. field_name_core_ absorbs that, and it
*      was added FOR this consolidation: NUL handling was the one capability
*      the two hand-rolled backend matchers had that the standard resolver
*      lacked. Without it the merge would have been a quiet downgrade. T1/T2
*      are what make that claim answerable instead of asserted.
*
*   2. THE X64 DESCRIPTOR TOKEN AS AN ALIAS. COURSE_TITLE_LONG is 17 bytes;
*      plan_x64_unique_fallback gives it the 10-byte token COURSE_TIT. The old
*      activeTagFieldIndex1() compared upper-cased logical names only and could
*      never have matched it. T3/T4 name the tag by the TOKEN and demand the
*      walk come back ordered by that field.
*
* THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE ROW, NOT THE VALUE. Every marker reads TAILKEY --
* six bytes, no truncation, no alias, nothing about its resolution in question
* -- so an arm can only go green by LANDING ON THE RIGHT ROW. The three orders
* disagree at both ends on purpose:
*
*     physical            T1  T2  T3      (as appended)
*     by STUDENT_LAST_NAME T2  T3  T1     (Anderson, Morgan, Wilson)
*     by COURSE_TITLE_LONG T3  T2  T1     (ALPHA, MIKE, ZULU)
*
* So a tag that resolved to the WRONG FIELD reads T2 where T3 is demanded, and
* an engine that accepted SET ORDER and walked physically anyway reads T1. The
* three worlds are separated by one comparison each.
*
* WHAT THIS FIRST CUT DOES NOT COVER, said here rather than left to be assumed:
*
*   - THE MANGLED TOKEN. Two fields whose first ten bytes collide get DOS-style
*     ~n aliases (COURSE_~1). No arm here builds that collision, so the
*     mangling branch of plan_x64_unique_fallback is UNPROVEN through the index
*     layer. It was left out deliberately: '~' in a tag argument is an
*     unmeasured question about the tokenizer, and putting an untested parse
*     inside the arm that proves the resolver would muddy both results.
*
*   - LOGICAL WINS OVER A COLLIDING TOKEN. Policy rule 1 says an authoritative
*     name beats an alias. Proving it needs a field whose logical name IS
*     another field's token, which is the same collision as above.
*
* Both want a second cut. Neither is covered by anything else in the corpus.
*
* THE THREE PATH SLOTS ARE ALL SET, AND THAT IS NOT BOILERPLATE. This file was
* written the day MWXSHAKE was found red because section 5B re-set DBF and
* INDEXES after a WORKSPACE SWITCH and left LMDB where DEFAULT's environment
* had put it (R131). A CDX on real disk is LMDB-backed; a wrong LMDB slot is
* `openCdx: LMDB env missing` and three markers reading the physical order.
*
* READ RULE: ten markers must PRINT and all ten read .T.
*   grep -c '^? "TAGF_'  <-- run this, read the run against THAT
* An errored marker prints NOTHING rather than going red, so COUNT them. If
* TAGF_G0/G1 are not both .T. the fixture is not what the arms assume and no
* T-arm below means anything.
*
* ON THE NAME. This spec was registered as `IDXNAME` on 2026-09-06 and renamed
* the same day, before it soaked, because the owner read it and had to ask
* whether it meant the IDX/INX family. It did not, and that was the whole
* problem: `.inx` IS a real index family here alongside CDX and CNX -- SO_ID.inx
* ships in INDEXES/x64 and SET ORDER reports `INX` as a container type -- so a
* spec id built from "IDX" plus "NAME" reads as a statement about that family
* rather than about field-NAME resolution. `TAGFIELD` says the thesis instead:
* A TAG IS A FIELD NAME. The table is `TAGFLD` and the markers are `TAGF_` for
* the same reason. Recorded rather than silently corrected, because the id
* appears under IDXNAME in commit 403cb4072 and a reader following that trail
* needs to land here.
*
* AND INX IS NOT A GAP THIS FILE LEAVES. Checked while renaming:
* cmd_setorder.cpp:33 states that INX is valid for SET INDEX attachment but
* that "tag activation via SET ORDER is intentionally not handled", and :816
* sends an INX caller to SET INDEX TO <file>.inx. INX carries ONE order and no
* tag directory, so there is no tag -> field question in it to answer wrongly.
* CDX and CNX are the entire surface, and both are covered here.
*
* Explicit-run until soaked.
* ===========================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX

&& THE TABLE, THE CONTAINER AND THE LMDB ENV IN ONE SWEEP. CDX CREATE refuses
&& to overwrite, so a leftover .cdx from a previous run would fail the build
&& and every arm under it.
ERASE TAGFLD CONFIRM

SELECT 0
CREATE X64 TAGFLD (STUDENT_LAST_NAME C(12), COURSE_TITLE_LONG C(12), TAILKEY C(6))
APPEND
REPLACE STUDENT_LAST_NAME WITH "Wilson"
REPLACE COURSE_TITLE_LONG WITH "ZULU"
REPLACE TAILKEY WITH "T1"
APPEND
REPLACE STUDENT_LAST_NAME WITH "Anderson"
REPLACE COURSE_TITLE_LONG WITH "MIKE"
REPLACE TAILKEY WITH "T2"
APPEND
REPLACE STUDENT_LAST_NAME WITH "Morgan"
REPLACE COURSE_TITLE_LONG WITH "ALPHA"
REPLACE TAILKEY WITH "T3"

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TAGF_G0 / G1 -- THE PHYSICAL ORDER, BEFORE ANY INDEX EXISTS.
*
* These are the baseline both T-pairs are measured against. If an engine ever
* walks physically while claiming an order, it lands back here.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

USE TAGFLD
TOP
? "TAGF_G0_physical_first_row_before_any_index:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T1")
BOTTOM
? "TAGF_G1_physical_last_row_before_any_index:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T3")

CDX CREATE

&& NAMED BY ITS FULL 17-BYTE LOGICAL NAME. This is the NUL-padding arm: the
&& name fits in char name[32] and comes back padded, so every reader of that
&& tag has to truncate at the NUL before comparing.
CDX ADDTAG STUDENT_LAST_NAME

&& NAMED BY ITS 10-BYTE DESCRIPTOR TOKEN, NOT ITS LOGICAL NAME. The field is
&& COURSE_TITLE_LONG. Only the x64 alias branch can connect the two.
CDX ADDTAG COURSE_TIT

CDX TAGS
BUILDLMDB YES

&& RE-OPEN BEFORE ORDERING. The container and its LMDB environment did not
&& exist when this area was first opened, and CDX attachment requires the
&& environment to exist. Re-opening is the honest way to get the attachment
&& the first USE could not have made.
USE TAGFLD

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TAGF_T1 / T2 -- A TAG NAMED BY A 17-BYTE LOGICAL NAME ORDERS THE WALK.
*
* Both ends move off the physical order: TOP goes T1 -> T2 and BOTTOM goes
* T3 -> T1. Green here is the whole NUL-padding claim, end to end, through
* ADDTAG, BUILDLMDB, SET ORDER and the walk.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SET ORDER TAG STUDENT_LAST_NAME
TOP
? "TAGF_T1_long_logical_name_orders_the_walk:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T2")
BOTTOM
? "TAGF_T2_and_the_far_end_of_that_order:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T1")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TAGF_T3 / T4 -- A TAG NAMED BY THE DESCRIPTOR TOKEN LANDS ON THE RIGHT FIELD.
*
* T3 IS THE ARM THIS FILE WAS WRITTEN FOR and it separates three worlds with
* one comparison:
*
*     T3  the token resolved to COURSE_TITLE_LONG   -- correct
*     T2  it resolved to STUDENT_LAST_NAME instead  -- wrong field
*     T1  the order was accepted and ignored        -- physical walk
*
* Nothing else in the corpus tells those three apart.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SET ORDER TAG COURSE_TIT
TOP
? "TAGF_T3_descriptor_token_orders_by_its_own_field:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T3")
BOTTOM
? "TAGF_T4_and_the_far_end_of_that_order:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T1")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TAGF_T5 -- THE ORDER SURVIVES A RETURN TO THE FIRST TAG.
*
* Switching back proves the second SET ORDER replaced the active tag rather
* than leaving the first one latched, which a resolver returning field 0 would
* look identical to at T3 if the engine simply kept the previous order.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SET ORDER TAG STUDENT_LAST_NAME
TOP
? "TAGF_T5_switching_back_re_resolves_the_first_tag:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T2")

* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TAGF_T6 -- A NAME THAT IS NEITHER A FIELD NOR A TOKEN IS REFUSED.
*
* TAGFNOSUCH matches no logical name and no descriptor token, so the resolver
* returns -1 and ADDTAG refuses at definition time. THE REFUSAL IS CONSOLE TEXT
* AND NO MARKER CAN READ IT -- the IDXDIFF precedent. Read the transcript for:
*
*     CDX ADDTAG: field not found: 'TAGFNOSUCH'.
*
* WHAT THE ARM CAN ASSERT is that the refusal changed NOTHING: the active order
* is still live afterwards. The tag directory it would have disturbed is the
* same one holding both working tags.
* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

CDX ADDTAG TAGFNOSUCH
TOP
? "TAGF_T6_refusal_left_the_live_order_alone:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T2")

* ===========================================================================
* SECTION 2 -- THE SAME QUESTION ON THE CNX BRANCH, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT GATE.
*
* EVERY ARM ABOVE WENT THROUGH cdx_has_tag(). SET ORDER TAG has TWO validation
* gates and they are not the same code: cmd_setorder.cpp:804 asks cdx_has_tag()
* for a CDX, and :811 asks cnx_has_tag() for a CNX. Only the second one walks
* area.fields() -- the first checks names in the container's tag directory --
* so cnx_has_tag() is a FIELD-NAME MATCHER and was the seventh in this lane.
*
* IT DISAGREED WITH THE RESOLVER AND THE DISAGREEMENT WAS REACHABLE. Until
* 2026-09-06 it compared up_copy(trim(f.name)) with no NUL handling and no x64
* descriptor-token alias, so on THIS EXACT SHAPE -- an x64 table with a CNX
* attached, ordered by a tag named with a field's 10-byte token -- the gate
* refused a tag the resolver accepts, and the refusal came first. SET ORDER
* answered "tag not available for CNX" about a tag that was available.
*
* CNX ON AN X64 TABLE IS LEGAL AND ATTACHING ONE IS EXPECTED TO ADVISE, NOT
* REFUSE (owner ruling 2026-08-09, and the AIF-099 note in cmd_setorder.cpp:
* an ATTACHED container wins tag resolution whichever legal family it is). An
* advisory line in the transcript here is the ruling working, not a fault.
*
* T7 IS THE DISCRIMINATOR FOR THE FIX. Before it, this arm cannot reach TOP at
* all -- SET ORDER refuses and the walk stays physical, reading T1. After it,
* the CNX orders by COURSE_TITLE_LONG and TOP is T3, the same row the CDX
* branch reached at T3. The two branches agreeing on the same token is the
* whole claim.
* ===========================================================================

CNX CREATE
CNX ADDTAG COURSE_TIT
REBUILD
SET INDEX TO TAGFLD.cnx
SET ORDER TAG COURSE_TIT

TOP
? "TAGF_T7_cnx_gate_accepts_the_descriptor_token:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T3")
BOTTOM
? "TAGF_T8_cnx_far_end_agrees_with_the_cdx_branch:" + (ALLTRIM(TAILKEY) = "T1")

CLOSE

* ===========================================================================
* TEARDOWN. ERASE sweeps the table, the container, its .cdx.meta sidecar and
* the LMDB env together -- all four live under the three slots set at the top,
* which is why they are set there and not inherited.
* ===========================================================================

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX
SET PATH INDEXES INDEXES/SANDBOX
SET PATH LMDB LMDB/SANDBOX
ERASE TAGFLD CONFIRM

DO x64
